<BaseLayout title="File server backup and recovery" description="How Nova Corp protects departmental data with Windows Server Backup and proves restoration." breadcrumbs={[{label:"NOVA-FILES01",href:"systems/nova-files01/"},{label:"Backup and recovery"}]}>
<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">File server / Windows Server Backup</div><h1>Backup and recovery</h1><p class="lead">The company can recover documents from a separate Windows backup target when a suitable recovery point exists.</p></div></section>
<section class="section compact"><div class="container grid-main"><article class="prose">
<h2>What does the backup protect?</h2><p>The documented Windows Server Backup policy protects the NovaData volume (D:) to NovaBackup (B:) using a daily 22:00 VSS Copy Backup schedule. It does not include bare-metal or System State recovery for this file server.</p>
<OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery ids={["fileserver-backup-gui","fileserver-backup-schedule"]} heading="Actual backup console and final recorded schedule"/>
<h2>Why does the company need it?</h2><p><strong>Problem:</strong> A document may disappear or be damaged when no suitable Previous Version exists. A list of backup files is not enough if the restore cannot actually be performed.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong> Retain recovery points on B:, monitor backup health and test file-level restoration to an alternate path. Do not overwrite live documents just to prove a restore.</p>
<h2>How the scheduled backup was proven</h2><p>CR-012 first tried Start-ScheduledTask on the built-in Windows Backup task. Windows refused because the task could not start on demand. An attempt to change the schedule while re-adding B: also failed because the target was already registered. The corrected acceptance test changed only the schedule:</p>
<pre><code>{'$TestTime = (Get-Date).AddMinutes(5).ToString("HH:mm")\nwbadmin enable backup -schedule:$TestTime -quiet'}</code></pre>
<p><strong>Expected:</strong> The scheduled task launches naturally and the backup completes, without a manual wbadmin start backup command.</p><p><strong>Actual:</strong> The journal records a start at 18:17:05, success at 18:17:19, and completion. The normal schedule was restored afterward.</p>
<OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery ids={["fileserver-scheduled-backup-pass"]} heading="Original scheduled-run event evidence"/>
<pre><code>{"wbadmin enable backup -schedule:22:00 -quiet\nwbadmin enable backup"}</code></pre>
<p>The recorded schedule was verified as 22:00 again. For a read-only recovery-point inspection, the journal used <code>wbadmin get versions -backupTarget:B:</code>.</p>
<h2>Prove that recovery works</h2><p>CR-012 restored a deleted file to an alternate path and compared SHA-256 fingerprints. The source remained absent from its live Finance location while the recovered file matched the original bytes. A separate CR-014 exercise took the backup disk offline, repaired it, ran a fresh backup and verified a recovered file.</p>
<OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery ids={["backup-original-recovered-hash","backup-disk-offline","backup-file-recovery","backup-sha256-match"]} heading="Recorded file recovery and integrity checks"/>
<h2>Related incident and recovery limits</h2><p><a href={base+"incidents/backup-target-unavailable/"}>Read the controlled backup-target failure →</a></p><p>B: is an attached Windows backup volume inside the nested lab. It is separate from D: but not an independent off-site disaster-recovery environment. A successful file restore does not prove a complete server or Domain Controller restoration.</p><p><a href={base+"files/shadow-copies/"}>Compare Shadow Copies and Previous Versions →</a></p>
